Assembler Version 11
====================
Assembled by: version 10 (both instgen and asm)

Features Supported by This Assembler
-------------------------------------
Major upgrade: supports standard 6502 assembly syntax with all addressing modes.
This is the first version that can assemble standard 6502 assembly language.

Syntax (new standard syntax):
- Standard 6502 mnemonic syntax: LDA #$42, STA $00, LDA ($10),Y, etc.
- Automatic addressing mode detection from operand syntax
- Automatic zero page detection: operand < $100 uses zero page mode
- All 13 standard 6502 addressing modes:
  - Implied: NOP, RTS, CLC, etc. (no operand)
  - Accumulator: ASL A, LSR A, ROL A, ROR A
  - Immediate: LDA #$42
  - Zero page: LDA $00
  - Zero page,X: LDA $00,X
  - Zero page,Y: LDX $00,Y
  - Absolute: LDA $1234
  - Absolute,X: LDA $1234,X
  - Absolute,Y: LDA $1234,Y
  - Indirect,X: LDA ($10,X)
  - Indirect,Y: LDA ($10),Y
  - Relative: BEQ label (branches)
  - Indirect: JMP ($1234)
- Character literals: #'A', #'\n', #'\\', #'\''
- DATA pseudo-instruction (backward compatible)
- Hex constants: $xx (1 byte) or $xxxx (2 bytes)
- Quoted strings with \n and \\ escapes
- Label definitions with PC value or explicit assignment
- PC setting via: * = $xxxx
- Low byte (<) and high byte (>) operators on labels
- Labels as operands in all addressing modes
- Comments with semicolons
- Two-pass assembly with forward reference tracking
- Forward reference tracking: labels used before definition are forced to
  absolute addressing mode (3 bytes) to prevent pass 1/pass 2 size mismatch
- .include directive
- .zeropage and .code section directives
- Command-line arguments: <assembler> <input> <output> [debug]
- Local labels with . prefix, scoped to enclosing global label

Directives:
- .include filename
- .zeropage - switches to zero page section
- .code - switches back to code section

Full 6502 instruction set (56 opcodes):
- Load/Store: LDA, LDX, LDY, STA, STX, STY
- Arithmetic: ADC, SBC
- Logical: AND, ORA, EOR
- Compare: CMP, CPX, CPY
- Bit test: BIT
- Increment/Decrement: INC, DEC, INX, INY, DEX, DEY
- Shift/Rotate: ASL, LSR, ROL, ROR
- Branch: BCC, BCS, BEQ, BMI, BNE, BPL, BVC, BVS
- Jump: JMP (absolute and indirect), JSR
- Stack: PHA, PHP, PLA, PLP
- Transfer: TAX, TAY, TSX, TXA, TXS, TYA
- Flag: CLC, CLD, CLI, CLV, SEC, SED, SEI
- Other: BRK, NOP, RTI, RTS
- Pseudo: DATA

Error handling: same as version 10 (errors 1-10), plus:
- Invalid addressing mode (error 11): mode not supported for instruction
- Invalid character literal (error 12): malformed character literal
- Too many forward references (error 13): forward reference list overflow
- Internal error - reference tracking (error 16): forward ref pointer mismatch
  between passes (debug verification)

New in this version (relative to version 10):
- Complete rewrite of instruction processing for standard 6502 syntax
- New instruction table format with mode:opcode pairs: each instruction
  entry contains the mnemonic followed by pairs of (mode, opcode) terminated
  by $FF. This replaces the old format where each mnemonic variant was a
  separate hash table entry.
- Addressing mode constants: MODE_NONE through MODE_IND, plus MODE_DATA
- New zero page variables: ADDR_MODE, INST_PTR_L/H, OPERAND_L/H,
  IS_FWDREF, FWDREF_PASS1_L/H
- INST_FLAG removed (replaced by mode-based dispatch)
- lookup_mnemonic: looks up 3-letter mnemonic in instruction hash table,
  returns pointer to mode:opcode list
- parse_operand_and_emit: parses operand to determine addressing mode and
  operand value, then finds matching opcode
- find_opcode_for_mode: walks mode:opcode pairs to find opcode matching
  the detected addressing mode, with ZP/ABS fallback
- check_if_branch: tests if instruction supports relative mode
- emit_instruction: emits opcode plus 0, 1, or 2 operand bytes based on mode
- Character literal parsing: #'x' syntax with escape sequences
- Comma and closing parenthesis are now token terminators (needed for
  parsing indexed and indirect addressing modes)
- check_for_data_pseudo: handles DATA as special case via MODE_DATA
- instgen generates the new-format instruction hash table
- Forward reference tracking for correct zero page detection:
  - IS_FWDREF flag: marks labels not yet defined in pass 1
  - FWDREF_LIST at $0200 (512 bytes): records PCs where forward-referenced
    labels caused zero page mode selection
  - handle_fwdref_mode: forces absolute addressing for forward refs in pass 1,
    uses FWDREF_LIST in pass 2 to match the same addressing mode choice
  - fwdref.asm module: forward reference list management (init, finalize,
    add_forward_ref, check_forward_ref, reset_fwdref_ptr)
  - Forward reference count verified between pass 1 and pass 2
  - Debug output includes forward reference count

Features Used in This Assembler's Source Code
----------------------------------------------
- Non-standard mnemonic syntax (LDA#, LDAZ, STAZ, BITZ, INCZ, ASLZ, etc.)
- DATA pseudo-instruction
- DATA $00 for zero page variable allocation in .zeropage section
- Hex constants ($xx and $xxxx)
- Quoted strings with escape sequences
- Labels and label assignments (label = value)
- PC setting (* = $2000 and * = $FFFC for vectors)
- Low byte (<) and high byte (>) operators
- Labels as absolute 2-byte operands
- Comments (;)
- BRK with inline error code and message (BRK $xx "message" $00)
- .include directive (out/inst.asm.out, environment.asm, common.asm,
  file_stack.asm, to_decimal.asm, fwdref.asm)
- .zeropage and .code section directives
- Local labels (.label syntax)
- Command-line arg access: argc, argv
- File I/O: open, close, read, openout, write

New syntax used in source (relative to version 10):
- None (source is still written in non-standard mnemonic syntax because it
  is assembled by version 10 which only supports non-standard syntax)

Other source changes:
- ADDR_MODE, INST_PTR_L/H, OPERAND_L/H added to zero page variables
- IS_FWDREF, FWDREF_PASS1_L/H added to zero page variables
- INST_FLAG removed (no longer needed with mode-based instruction lookup)
- fwdref.asm module for forward reference list management
- Substantially larger codebase due to operand parsing, mode detection,
  character literal parsing, and forward reference tracking logic
